          "case_name": "Suter v. Artist M.",
          "citation": "503 U.S. 347 (1992)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1992,
          "date_decided": "1992-03-25",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that the Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act's reasonable efforts requirement creates no privately enforceable right, so beneficiaries cannot sue to enforce it under the Act itself or under 42 U.S.C. 1983.",
          "relevance": "This is the controlling federal authority that a state's failure to meet Title IV-E plan requirements is remedied through federal funding mechanisms, not private suit.",
          "quote": "We hold that the Act does not create an enforceable right on behalf of respondents.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112712/suter-v-artist-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112712
        },
        {
          "case_name": "People ex rel. J.S.B., Jr.",
          "citation": "2005 S.D. 3, 691 N.W.2d 611",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 2005,
          "date_decided": "2005-01-05",
          "holding": "The court held that ASFA's aggravated-circumstances exception to reasonable efforts does not override ICWA's active efforts requirement, but affirmed termination because DSS in fact continued to provide active efforts that proved unsuccessful.",
          "relevance": "It holds that a state agency cannot use one federal statute's exception to excuse noncompliance with another federal statute's mandate.",
          "quote": "Under ICWA, DSS was bound by law to make \"active efforts\" to reunite J.S.B with his father, but it was not required to persist with futile efforts.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/901424/the-people-of-the-state-of-south-dakota-in-the-interest-of-jsb-jr/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 901424
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Haaland v. Brackeen",
          "citation": "599 U.S. 255 (2023)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 2023,
          "date_decided": "2023-06-15",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court rejected every challenge to the Indian Child Welfare Act, upholding it against Article I and anticommandeering attacks on the merits and disposing of the equal protection and nondelegation claims for lack of standing.",
          "relevance": "It settles that a state cannot decline to apply ICWA on federalism grounds, so noncompliance is a compliance failure rather than a contested constitutional question.",
          "quote": "the bottom line is that we reject all of petitioners' challenges to the statute, some on the merits and others for lack of standing",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9402136/haaland-v-brackeen/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 9406660
        }
